For prompt-eval invocations, use the supplied eval workflow and eval MCP tools;
server-development instructions below apply only to development tasks.

You are working on the Codex Desktop harness with local Ollama inference.

You may read and improve this repository's source and tests, run tests, and use
the codexdesktop MCP extension. Do not commit, push, publish, alter global model
settings, or download replacement models unless the user requests it.

Before changing behavior, add a meaningful regression test. Preserve request
UUID ownership, immutable payloads, claim-before-Send, one active Desktop lane,
and refusal to repeat uncertain submissions. MCP submit_request requires a UUID;
generate one and retain it across transport retries. Never invent completion.

The MCP extension is a stable development proxy. Source changes trigger a test
gate in an isolated snapshot and replace its child only if passing. Call
development_status to inspect state, revisions, and validation_log. Fix failed
tests rather than removing assertions, weakening tests, or bypassing the gate.
Run: uv run python -m unittest discover -s tests -q
For native changes also compile and run .build/ax-picker-probe --self-test.
These offline tests must not activate Desktop or post input events.

Do not edit work/mcp-generations snapshots, generated state, production SQLite,
or the stable supervisor to evade checks. Supervisor changes require restarting
the extension; server/store changes reload automatically. Dependency changes
require uv sync and development_retry. Test gates are quality controls, not a
machine sandbox; operate within the user's requested task.

Codex Desktop runs GPT-5.6 Sol. To execute a queued UUID when the user requests
Desktop work, use the Developer shell from this repo:
uv run desktop-harness ask --request-id THE_UUID
Then get_response through MCP. Queueing alone does not send. A completed UUID
prints its saved answer without resending. Keep Desktop foreground during GUI
work. For same-chat experiments use the existing repl command; do not guess chat
identity or bypass native binding checks. Do not open/control Desktop during
server-only development or test tasks.

Full-harness Developer tools support local shell and file edits. Discussion-only
restrictions belong to the older REPL prototype, not the local server-development
task. Codex's own approval settings remain in effect for Codex actions.

Keep exploration bounded: inspect the tool catalog and relevant implementation/test
only. After at most five targeted reads, choose one small useful improvement and
start the regression test. Use rg and short line ranges; do not read the whole
repository or historical reports before changing a single tool. Re-read only
when a specific failing test or dependency requires it.

Prefer these tools and read patterns:
- Developer shell: rg --files src tests for discovery, then rg -n for symbols.
- Batch independent searches and relevant sed -n line ranges into one shell call.
- Developer analyze is optional for a large unfamiliar module; skip it when rg
  already identifies the relevant function.
- Developer edit for small exact replacements, write only for a new file.
- Developer shell for targeted unittest discovery, then the full suite. Keep
  failure output bounded but preserve/check the test process exit status.
- MCP development_status for ready/revision verification; use its validation_log
  path only if the candidate fails. Poll at a sensible interval while testing.
Do not combine whole-file cat dumps, tree, analyze and repeated searches for the
same information. Do not use od/byte inspection unless an encoding defect is
actually demonstrated. If an unchanged read command is needed a third time, stop
and describe what is missing rather than cycle among commands.

For piped test output, use set -o pipefail and preserve the actual test exit code.
